Neutrino astronomy stands as a pivotal area of research, with the detection of electron antineutrinos ($ ar{
u}_e$) from supernovae being particularly significant. The Deep Underground Neutrino Experiment (DUNE) is uniquely positioned to observe such events, provided that the neutrino oscillation parameters are known. This study examines the capabilities of DUNE to detect $ ar{
u}_e$ originating from a supernova located in the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC). We analyze the event yields and the energy distribution of detected neutrinos under two oscillation scenarios: the standard normal hierarchy (NH) and the inverted hierarchy (IH). Our findings indicate that DUNE is capable of identifying approximately 1,000 $ ar{
u}_e$ events from a supernova in the LMC, with the expected event numbers and spectral shapes differing between the NH and IH cases.

The LHCb experiment has identified intriguing deviations in the $R_{K^{*}}$ and $R_{K}$ ratios, as well as in the angular observable $P_{5}^{\prime}$, which may hint at physics beyond the Standard Model. This paper investigates the impact of a new physics contribution to the $ ar{b}	o c ar{
u}_{c}\ell ar{
u}_{\ell}$ process on these observed anomalies. We demonstrate that a new physics scenario involving a new gauge boson $X$ coupled to the $ ar{b}	o c\ell
u_{\ell}$ transition can simultaneously address the $R_{K^{*}}$ and $R_{K}$ discrepancies, the $P_{5}^{\prime}$ anomaly, and the recently reported anomaly in the $ ar{b}	o c\ell ar{
u}_{\ell}$ decay channel.
